WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.280
Each year, 40 million students graduate&nbsp;
high school and make what many consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.800
the most important decision of one’s&nbsp;
entire life: where to attend college.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.880
Anxious about their future,&nbsp;
pressured by their parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.560
and armed with only the most basic&nbsp;
understanding of compound interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.440
would-be college freshmen make the kind&nbsp;
of customer most businesses only dream of.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.040
An 18-year-old is more informed about the&nbsp;
brand of shoes they wear than the institution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.160
at which they’ll spend 200 weeks of&nbsp;
their lives and $84,000 on average.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.160
It’s not their fault.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.720
The basic problem is information asymmetry.&nbsp;
Prospective students can take tours, deciding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.320
whether a school’s trees are a satisfactory shade&nbsp;
of green and buildings sufficiently old looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.960
but nearly everything else they know&nbsp;
comes from just one source: the seller.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.760
Now imagine making this decision 7,000 miles away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.680
having never stepped foot in the country,&nbsp;
and as a non-native English speaker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.840
That’s the challenge faced by America’s&nbsp;
340,000 Chinese international students.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.640
So, what does a diligent&nbsp;
young adult do to compensate?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.680
They turn to the Consumer Reports&nbsp;
of education: college rankings.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.080
But for this prudence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.400
little do they know, they’re rewarded&nbsp;
with a mix of fraud and deception.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.200
It’s not just that rankings are inaccurate or&nbsp;
misleading — though make no mistake, they are!

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.960
The real problem is much, much deeper.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.960
In fact, almost everything wrong with higher&nbsp;
education today — the student loan crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.040
inequitable admissions, academic plagiarism, and&nbsp;
the collapse of tenure are ultimately symptoms.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.760
…Symptoms of one giant,&nbsp;
interconnected web of deceit.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.720
Sponsored by Fabulous, the app&nbsp;
that uses behavioral science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.040
to help you build healthy habits like&nbsp;
meditation, exercise, and better sleep.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.560
From the day a baby is born in China,&nbsp;
he or she unknowingly enters a race.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.040
Although they won’t take the Gaokao, China’s&nbsp;
college entrance exam, for another 18 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.960
it alone will ultimately determine&nbsp;
their career and social class.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.320
And “determine” means determine — there are no&nbsp;
letters of recommendation, extracurriculars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.600
awards, or alternate pathways. The&nbsp;
Gaokao is the only race that matters.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.720
Because it’s held just once a year&nbsp;
and the results highly publicized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.960
the cost of failure is enormous.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.720
Every hour of sleep lost, every&nbsp;
hobby forgone, and talent abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.840
is easily justified — it could be the&nbsp;
difference between one’s dream and fallback.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.000
Why do Chinese students collectively sacrifice&nbsp;
what the rest of the world calls “adolescence”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.880
for a single exam? In a word: fairness. The&nbsp;
poor farmer’s daughter and chief executive’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.640
son take the same exam, in the&nbsp;
same room, at the same time.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.840
Of course, in the real world, the rich and&nbsp;
powerful usually find a way. Contradictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.320
after all, have never stopped China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.080
a “Communist” country with the 2nd&nbsp;
highest concentration of billionaires.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:27.200
For middle and upper-class kids, an overseas&nbsp;
education is a lifeboat on the Titanic — an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.040
escape from academic pressure and&nbsp;
the public spotlight, respectively.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.080
Hong Kong Chief Executive Carrie Lam’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.920
son and Chairman Xi Jinping’s&nbsp;
daughter both attended Harvard.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.360
Now, hold that thought for a moment.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.200
Seven in ten Americans attend a public college.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.840
But despite coughing up over 21,000 US dollars&nbsp;
a year for in-state tuition, room, and board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:59.840
on average, students themselves only directly&nbsp;
contribute one-fifth of their school’s budget.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.320
Most of the total — about&nbsp;
40% — is covered by state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.320
federal, and a bit of local government&nbsp;
grants, contracts, and appropriations.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.000
What this means is that public universities&nbsp;
are largely at the whim of their federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.640
and state governments, who, in turn,&nbsp;
depend on a healthy stream of tax revenue.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:26.960
During “normal” times — which is to say, a long&nbsp;
time ago — this was no big deal. The system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:34.160
imperfect as it was, chugged along mostly…&nbsp;
fine… enough until the 2008 recession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.160
when schools were painfully reminded of&nbsp;
the house of cards they’re built upon.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.880
People lost their jobs, the&nbsp;
government lost its tax revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.400
and thus universities lost their funding. Oops.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.440
Not only was there just less money&nbsp;
going around, but because there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:56.080
less money going around, universities were&nbsp;
under greater scrutiny to use it well.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.840
Less money, yet more demands.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.080
So what did they do?

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.760
What every good Capitalist does, of&nbsp;
course! They commodified education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:11.840
casting their line further and further&nbsp;
afield in search of untapped markets.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.440
They both exported the product — slapping&nbsp;
their logos on deep-pocketed modular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.120
architecture everywhere — and imported customers.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.160
International students weren’t some&nbsp;
new invention but universities were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.160
shocked by how deep the wells&nbsp;
were — particularly in China.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.800
It all began as a short-term&nbsp;
solution to an acute problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.840
fill a hole in the budget. But&nbsp;
they soon realized the potential.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.600
The intense pressure of the Gaokao, combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.280
China’s growing middle-class had produced an&nbsp;
insatiable desire for overseas credentials.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.640
Today, there are two and half times as many&nbsp;
Chinese international students in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:01.120
as second place India, which is itself two&nbsp;
point seven times larger than third place Korea.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.840
And that’s not just because China&nbsp;
is huge. The country is home to 18%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.960
of the world’s population, but 35%&nbsp;
of international students in America.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.680
Demand, in other words, had accidentally&nbsp;
discovered a huge, under-exploited supply.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.040
There was basically no limit to the number&nbsp;
of keen Chinese students they could attract.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.960
Even a decent state school in the middle of&nbsp;
nowhere could arbitrage the American brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:35.280
converting its U.S. address plus the vague notion&nbsp;
of “international exposure” into cold hard cash.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.840
But, say you’re a Chinese student who’s never&nbsp;
stepped foot in the States. How do you pick a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:48.080
school despite knowing next to nothing about the&nbsp;
country, much less any individual city or state?

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:54.240
You take out your phone, Google “best colleges&nbsp;
in America” and tap on the first result, duh!

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.200
Enter college rankings.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.200
Students and their parents are universally&nbsp;
desperate for objective answers — guidance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.840
in making the quote “right choice”, and&nbsp;
later assurance that they indeed have.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.400
Rankings prey on this anxiety, and doubly&nbsp;
so in the case of international students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.760
whose vacuum of knowledge&nbsp;
is ripe for exploitation.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.640
Chinese international students,&nbsp;
in particular, are both, by far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:31.200
the largest kind, and, according to this survey,&nbsp;
the most persuaded by reputation and prestige.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:37.840
The only little problem is that rankings&nbsp;
are…well, complete and utter garbage.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.200
Let’s start with the low-hanging fruit:&nbsp;
It isn’t possible to reduce an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.480
university to a single number. At least, not&nbsp;
without losing all meaning in the process.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.640
In what world does Goshen College,&nbsp;
a Mennonite school with 827 students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.400
in rural Indiana belong on the same list as&nbsp;
a party school, like say, the University of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.040
Alabama, whose student population is&nbsp;
larger than the entire town of Goshen?

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.360
In fairness, they do share&nbsp;
the same basic mission — the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.520
proliferation of knowledge. But the&nbsp;
same can’t be said of all schools.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.680
The Mennonites look like close relatives of&nbsp;
Crimson Tide when compared to Peking University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:26.000
— whose charter replaced “freedom of thought”&nbsp;
with loyalty to the Communist Party in 2019.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.760
One building strives to cultivate&nbsp;
the next generation of thinkers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.160
The other to churn out obedient workers.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.080
Yet the “big three” international rankings&nbsp;
— QS, ARWU, and The Times — don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.040
acknowledge the fact that entire fields of&nbsp;
study are forbidden at some universities.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.440
But, okay, enough with all&nbsp;
the high-minded philosophy.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.280
At the end of the day, people like to rank things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.240
And there is an undeniable difference&nbsp;
between your local community college and MIT.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.360
Assuming that schools can be reduced to a single&nbsp;
number, what specific criteria should be used?

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:07.680
You might suggest student&nbsp;
satisfaction, teaching quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.760
graduate employment rate, or, how&nbsp;
could you forget, the cost of tuition!

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.600
Wrong, wrong, wrong, and lol.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:20.800
What rankings do consider is the&nbsp;
proportion of international students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.360
which brings us to the positive feedback loop.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:29.120
Each new international student increases&nbsp;
their school’s ratio, which, in turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:34.480
boosts its overall ranking. Meanwhile, how&nbsp;
do international students choose schools?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:39.360
That’s right, rankings!&nbsp;
Around, and around, and around.

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:46.320
At some point, to reject international students is&nbsp;
to willfully forgo prestige, and thereby revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.680
This irresistible self-perpetuating&nbsp;
cycle encourages schools to admit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.240
students even with an inadequate grasp&nbsp;
of English, setting them up to fail.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.400
Schools that pursue this strategy tend to&nbsp;
have very large Chinese student populations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:09.440
which often results in “social bubbles” on campus.&nbsp;
After 4 years, they may graduate with few, if any,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:14.000
local friends, despite coming, in&nbsp;
large part, for that “exposure”.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.560
Now, to put things in perspective, the&nbsp;
international student ratio only comprises 5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:27.840
of the QS ranking formula. But make no mistake,&nbsp;
their actual contribution is many times larger.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.840
Few Americans pay the price&nbsp;
of tuition shown on Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.800
Those who attend college in their own state&nbsp;
pay only about half the sticker price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.080
and even out-of-state tuition&nbsp;
is offset by financial aid.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:47.200
About the only students who do pay full price are&nbsp;
international students. And on top of that, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:52.800
pay additional “fees”, disproportionately live&nbsp;
in student dorms, and buy university meal plans.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.080
With all that extra cash, schools&nbsp;
can buy their way up the rankings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.640
attracting even more students, and so on.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:07.120
In 2008, Baylor offered new students $1,000&nbsp;
a year in student aid to retake their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:11.760
SATs and score an extra 50 points,&nbsp;
artificially boosting its numbers.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.360
QS even offers a paid consulting&nbsp;
service, in a clear conflict of interest.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.800
In the best case, this extra cash is&nbsp;
spent hiring more teachers — increasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.920
its teacher-student ratio, which&nbsp;
comprises 20% of the QS formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:32.960
and attracting researchers, who boost the&nbsp;
school’s academic citations — another 20%.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.000
Now, you might argue that’s how things are&nbsp;
supposed to work. Money affords quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.040
The rich get richer — no surprise there, and&nbsp;
there’s no reason schools should all be equal.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.400
But what does “quality” really mean?

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.280
Teacher-student ratios tell you&nbsp;
how small a school’s classes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.840
but not how good the teaching.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:00.320
Students may wonder why so many of their&nbsp;
classes are capped at 19 or 20 students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.040
The reason is that U.S. News counts&nbsp;
the number of classes with 20 or fewer.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:10.320
The result is that access to the&nbsp;
best professors is restricted.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:15.360
In a world with an infinite number of great&nbsp;
teachers, smaller class sizes would always be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:20.800
better. But, as anyone who's attended college&nbsp;
can tell you, that’s definitely not the case.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:28.640
Citations per faculty is an even worse proxy for&nbsp;
quality. The number of academic publications a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:33.040
school produces has almost nothing to&nbsp;
do with the undergraduate experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:38.320
In fact, more time spent on research&nbsp;
equals less emphasis on teaching.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:44.240
When folks reminisce about college, they&nbsp;
mention things like the fun social environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.720
and maybe one or two stand-out classes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:51.840
What they don’t tend to remember is their&nbsp;
school’s number of academic citations.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.040
Silly as this sounds, millions of students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.320
inadvertently choose schools&nbsp;
based on this very criteria.

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:03.120
Yet just 2% of admissions directors&nbsp;
think ranking systems are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.480
quote, “very effective” at helping&nbsp;
students find the right school.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:13.600
All they really do is reward those willing&nbsp;
to play the game, like Singapore’s NTU — who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:18.000
made massive leaps in the rankings thanks&nbsp;
to a sudden, coordinated burst of research.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:24.320
…That’s, of course, assuming the research being&nbsp;
cited is even good. You can probably think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:28.880
a widely-cited paper linking vaccines&nbsp;
to a certain… neurological disorder.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.520
Now, you might counter that&nbsp;
students are just holding it wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:37.120
misunderstanding what rankings&nbsp;
actually measure (research).

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:39.760
But they aren’t even good at measuring that!

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.240
A full 50% of the QS formula is reputation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:49.280
How does a school gain a “reputation”?&nbsp;
In no small part, rankings!

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:54.960
In other words, rankings measure reputation,&nbsp;
and reputations are determined by ranking.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:00.960
Professor Imanol Ordorika calls rankings&nbsp;
“Harvardometers” — suggesting they merely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:07.120
measure how close a school is to what we already&nbsp;
assume to be the best. Any formula that didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:11.280
place Harvard near the top would have to&nbsp;
go back to the drawing board until it did.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.840
Still, somehow it gets worse. Way worse.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.800
The way reputation is measured is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.280
in part, with online surveys in which&nbsp;
respondents are rewarded with free “swag”.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:30.080
U.S. News has college presidents to rate hundreds&nbsp;
of their competitors on a scale of one to five.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:35.280
In one study, Princeton’s business school was&nbsp;
ranked in the top 10 despite not even existing.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.720
Meanwhile, according to researchers, rankings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.880
aren’t even precise enough to accurately&nbsp;
distinguish between first and tenth place.

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:46.240
The problem is that we act as though they can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:52.000
In one egregious case, administrators of&nbsp;
the University of Malaya were fired after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:56.640
it suddenly dropped 80 places in The Times&nbsp;
ranking due only to a change in definition.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:03.040
So why not group schools together to at least&nbsp;
avoid giving the false impression of precision?

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:09.280
Because this volatility is a core part of the&nbsp;
rankings business model — it produces an annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:16.080
round of drama and publicity. “Wow, this school&nbsp;
dropped 10 places!” and “this one gained 5!”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.600
when nothing effectively changed&nbsp;
as far as students are concerned.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:28.320
So. Colleges can’t be reduced to a single number.&nbsp;
Even if they could, this isn’t how you’d do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:32.640
And even if it were, you wouldn’t add&nbsp;
all these different metrics together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.080
producing a result that neither accurately&nbsp;
measures reputation, quality, or research.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:44.080
But remember, rankings are not just&nbsp;
a corporate parasite one can ignore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:50.080
Imagine if Consumer Reports had a total&nbsp;
monopoly on public information about phones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:55.600
TVs, or cars. Apple and Toyota would&nbsp;
design their products not based on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:01.760
we actually want but whatever arbitrary metrics&nbsp;
profit-seeking Consumer Reports decides on.

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:07.280
We’ve talked about schools that benefit&nbsp;
from this system. But for every Harvard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:12.400
NTU, and Princeton, a dozen more are&nbsp;
held totally captive against their will.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:16.560
Ranking companies enforce&nbsp;
compliance with a threat:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.640
Either hand over the data we demand,&nbsp;
or your rankings will suffer.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.960
That’s no exaggeration. U.S. News, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:29.120
assigns schools who refuse to play&nbsp;
the game below-average scores.

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:34.320
Ultimately, everyone suffers.&nbsp;
Professors are incentivized to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:38.160
farm citations to keep their&nbsp;
jobs, students are deceived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:42.960
international students totally exploited,&nbsp;
and administrators encouraged to cheat.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:50.800
Yet we all actively play the game. Colleges whose&nbsp;
very professors write papers eviscerating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:55.920
methodology of rankings continue advertising&nbsp;
their U.S. News ranking on pamphlets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:00.640
Even you, watching this very&nbsp;
video, may intellectually believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.600
rankings are pointless but will surely close&nbsp;
this window and continue acting otherwise.

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:12.400
It’s a kind of psychological pyramid scheme. Even&nbsp;
if you don’t believe in rankings, you’d be wise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:17.840
to act as though you do because, real or fake,&nbsp;
they really do mean something to other people.

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:24.000
To justify our crushing student loans and because&nbsp;
you’d be dumb not to advertise the “good” school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:29.840
you attended, we recruit others — perpetuating&nbsp;
the belief that rankings mean… anything.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:33.440
But things may be about to change.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:38.160
Remember: at the heart of all&nbsp;
this were international students.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:44.720
Just a few short years ago, the rapid growth&nbsp;
of Chinese students on U.S. campuses produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:50.400
a mix of both ugly racist anxiety, and&nbsp;
legitimate concerns about academic freedom.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.480
Schools had found a seemingly&nbsp;
bottomless source of income.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.840
To them it felt like an infinite money glitch.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:03.920
And when every cast of the line is even more&nbsp;
fruitful than the last, even the most rational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:09.280
fisherman will struggle to restrain themselves.&nbsp;
How could they deliberately leave all this free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:14.640
money on the table? And besides, if they&nbsp;
don’t catch these fish, someone else will.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:20.080
Thus, they continued to depend more and&nbsp;
more on Chinese international students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:24.560
even while aware of the risk of relying&nbsp;
so heavily on one single market.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.840
Today, the concern is reversed.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:31.520
When national borders closed in the Spring of 2020&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:35.680
and restrictions extended into summer,&nbsp;
universities began wondering if they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:39.280
just permanently lost a critical&nbsp;
component of their revenue model.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:42.960
The decline in international students doesn’t look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:48.880
catastrophic. That is, until you focus on&nbsp;
the number of new international students.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:54.320
Existing students are likely to finish their&nbsp;
degrees no matter what, and because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.320 --> 00:19:00.640
decision to take the SAT over the Gaokao, for&nbsp;
instance, is made years in advance in China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:06.560
schools still have a few years of runway left.&nbsp;
Students who’ve already committed to this path.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:11.440
The concern is that, while most of&nbsp;
the world will return to normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:16.240
the largest and most lucrative source&nbsp;
of students, China, never will.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:21.360
Because, for them, COVID is only&nbsp;
one in an already growing list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:24.800
concerns about studying, or even living, abroad.

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:30.640
The Chinese government increasingly portrays&nbsp;
the world beyond its borders as a violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.840
unwelcoming, apocalyptic wasteland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:39.520
seizing on every legitimately tragic crime in&nbsp;
order to tell a larger story of state collapse.

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:45.200
The number one criteria for choosing&nbsp;
a school is not teaching quality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:50.080
career prospects, or even&nbsp;
cost of living. It’s, by far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:54.880
“safety”. And international students&nbsp;
have never felt less safe in America.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.920
What does it mean for higher education&nbsp;
that a central feature of their business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:04.000
academic, and incentive structure&nbsp;
may be permanently disrupted?

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:08.640
That their largest source of revenue&nbsp;
may suddenly be gone forever?

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:11.920
How will they compensate when this wasteful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.760
inefficient, system also&nbsp;
becomes financially insolvent?

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:20.960
The truth is no one yet knows. Not even they do.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:27.120
Some argue the “university” model is in&nbsp;
decline and will soon be replaced with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:32.640
mass online education. But schools&nbsp;
still have one giant advantage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:39.920
Anything can be learned online, but only schools&nbsp;
can offer accountability, structure, and guidance.

